WEBVTT

00:00:00.086 --> 00:00:03.805
A core metacognitive skill is knowing what you know, what you partly know,

00:00:03.805 --> 00:00:05.080
and what you do not know yet.

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:07.180
This sounds simple, but it is often difficult.

00:00:07.180 --> 00:00:09.420
People can feel confident because a topic is familiar,

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:13.305
because the explanation sounded clear, or because they have seen the same words before.

00:00:13.305 --> 00:00:17.440
That feeling can be useful, but it is not proof of usable understanding.

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:19.280
Familiarity and mastery are different.

00:00:19.280 --> 00:00:21.440
Familiarity means something feels recognizable.

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:24.130
Mastery means you can explain, retrieve, apply, compare,

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:26.995
or adapt the idea when the source is not directly in front of you.

00:00:26.995 --> 00:00:31.320
A fluent explanation can hide weak comprehension if the person has not tested the idea

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:32.480
through examples,

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:34.240
application, or recall.

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:37.160
Confidence can rise faster than competence when feedback is missing.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:39.610
Illusions of understanding are common in learning and work.

00:00:39.610 --> 00:00:43.280
A person may believe they understand a system because they can follow a diagram while

00:00:43.280 --> 00:00:44.595
someone else explains it.

00:00:44.595 --> 00:00:46.480
Later, when asked to troubleshoot the system,

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:49.580
they may discover that the relationships were never secure in memory.

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:50.995
The issue is not intelligence.

00:00:50.995 --> 00:00:53.480
The issue is that recognition felt like capability.

00:00:53.480 --> 00:00:56.200
To test understanding, ask the idea to do work.

00:00:56.200 --> 00:00:57.440
Explain it in your own words.

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:00.315
Apply it to a new example.

00:01:00.315 --> 00:01:01.715
Retrieve the steps without looking.

00:01:01.715 --> 00:01:04.080
Compare two similar cases and explain the difference.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:05.590
Teach the idea to someone else.

00:01:05.590 --> 00:01:06.890
These tests make gaps visible,

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:10.440
and visible gaps are useful because they tell you what to learn or practice next.
